{"reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting in DeBERTa training (val loss rises after epoch 3) and suboptimal Logistic Regression performance (val log loss 0.5141). The initial plan's core direction addresses these via cross-validation, improved training strategy, and better n-gram features. Historical experiments confirm that cross-validation (5-fold) with DeBERTa reduces variance and improves generalization, while deeper fine-tuning with lower LR and cosine schedule stabilizes training. Enhanced n-gram features (chi-squared selection) elevate LR's contribution to the ensemble. Therefore, I select all three modules: 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to refine n-gram features, 'model_design' to implement cross-validation and deeper fine-tuning architecture, and 'training_evaluation' to incorporate these changes in the training loop.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Add chi-squared feature selection (k=10000) to combined TF-IDF char (1-5 gram, max_features=5000) and word (1-3 gram, max_features=5000) n-grams, plus punctuation sequence features (3-5 char n-grams, max_features=500). WHY: Current n-grams (13500 features) produce LR log loss 0.5141, underperforming DeBERTa and XGBoost. Chi-squared selection retains only informative features, reducing noise and boosting LR to ~0.30 val loss, improving ensemble balance. Historical experiments show this feature selection step successfully lifts LR. HOW: After stacking n-gram matrices, apply SelectKBest(chi2, k=10000) on training sparse matrix (scaled to non-negative via MaxAbsScaler), then transform val and test set, keep results as sparse arrays for LR training.", "model_design": "WHAT: Replace single train/val split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa feature extraction. Train 5 separate DeBERTa models (each on 4 folds), extract [CLS] embeddings from each fold's validation samples (out-of-fold), and average embeddings from 5 models for test set. Use the same hyperparameters as initial plan but with gradient accumulation steps=2 and cosine LR schedule with warmup. WHY: Single split wastes 10% training data and high variance leads to overfitting (best epoch 3). Historical experiments show 5-fold CV improves val log loss by 0.01-0.03 by using all data for training and generating more robust embeddings. Gradient accumulation (effective batch 32) and cosine schedule stabilize training, preventing rapid overfitting seen from epoch 4. HOW: Implement StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, fold-by-fold: tokenize, fine-tune DeBERTa (same architecture but lower LR=1e-5, cosine scheduler with 10% warmup, 6 epochs per fold), extract embeddings, aggregate OOF train embeddings (concatenate across folds) and average test embeddings. Preserve existing model backbone and tokenizer.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Modify training loop to incorporate 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa, including fold-level early stopping and model saving. Use cosine LR scheduler with linear warmup for 6 epochs per fold, gradient accumulation steps=2, and LR=1e-5. Preserve label smoothing, gradient clipping, and mixed precision. After obtaining OOF embeddings, retrain XGBoost and LR on full OOF embeddings (concatenated across all folds) and handcrafted features. Optimize ensemble weights via grid search over validation log loss as before. Generate final submission from ensemble. WHY: Cross-validation requires orchestration of per-fold training and embedding aggregation. The existing single-split training loop must be extended to iterate over folds while preserving interface (train/val/test data structures). Historical experiments show that OOF embeddings improve generalizability. Lower LR and cosine schedule address the overfitting issue at epoch 4+. HOW: In a new function, loop over StratifiedKFold folds, for each fold: create DataLoaders, train DeBERTa with gradient accumulation (accumulate gradients over 2 batches before optimizer step), use cosine LR scheduler (torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingLR with warmup via linear increase), early stopping on val loss with patience 3. After all folds, concatenate OOF embeddings, train XGBoost and LR on combined features (handcrafted + OOF DeBERTa), and generate predictions as before. Preserve existing submission format and file paths."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting in DeBERTa training (val loss rises after epoch 3) and suboptimal Logistic Regression performance (val log loss 0.5141). The initial plan's core direction addresses these via cross-validation, improved training strategy, and better n-gram features. Historical experiments confirm that cross-validation (5-fold) with DeBERTa reduces variance and improves generalization, while deeper fine-tuning with lower LR and cosine schedule stabilizes training. Enhanced n-gram features (chi-squared selection) elevate LR's contribution to the ensemble. Therefore, I select all three modules: 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to refine n-gram features, 'model_design' to implement cross-validation and deeper fine-tuning architecture, and 'training_evaluation' to incorporate these changes in the training loop.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Add chi-squared feature selection (k=10000) to combined TF-IDF char (1-5 gram, max_features=5000) and word (1-3 gram, max_features=5000) n-grams, plus punctuation sequence features (3-5 char n-grams, max_features=500). WHY: Current n-grams (13500 features) produce LR log loss 0.5141, underperforming DeBERTa and XGBoost. Chi-squared selection retains only informative features, reducing noise and boosting LR to ~0.30 val loss, improving ensemble balance. Historical experiments show this feature selection step successfully lifts LR. HOW: After stacking n-gram matrices, apply SelectKBest(chi2, k=10000) on training sparse matrix (scaled to non-negative via MaxAbsScaler), then transform val and test set, keep results as sparse arrays for LR training.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace single train/val split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation for DeBERTa feature extraction. Train 5 separate DeBERTa models (each on 4 folds), extract [CLS] embeddings from each fold's validation samples (out-of-fold), and average embeddings from 5 models for test set. Use the same hyperparameters as initial plan but with gradient accumulation steps=2 and cosine LR schedule with warmup. WHY: Single split wastes 10% training data and high variance leads to overfitting (best epoch 3). Historical experiments show 5-fold CV improves val log loss by 0.01-0.03 by using all data for training and generating more robust embeddings. Gradient accumulation (effective batch 32) and cosine schedule stabilize training, preventing rapid overfitting seen from epoch 4. HOW: Implement StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, fold-by-fold: tokenize, fine-tune DeBERTa (same architecture but lower LR=1e-5, cosine scheduler with 10% warmup, 6 epochs per fold), extract embeddings, aggregate OOF train embeddings (concatenate across folds) and average test embeddings. Preserve existing model backbone and tokenizer.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Modify training loop to incorporate 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa, including fold-level early stopping and model saving. Use cosine LR scheduler with linear warmup for 6 epochs per fold, gradient accumulation steps=2, and LR=1e-5. Preserve label smoothing, gradient clipping, and mixed precision. After obtaining OOF embeddings, retrain XGBoost and LR on full OOF embeddings (concatenated across all folds) and handcrafted features. Optimize ensemble weights via grid search over validation log loss as before. Generate final submission from ensemble. WHY: Cross-validation requires orchestration of per-fold training and embedding aggregation. The existing single-split training loop must be extended to iterate over folds while preserving interface (train/val/test data structures). Historical experiments show that OOF embeddings improve generalizability. Lower LR and cosine schedule address the overfitting issue at epoch 4+. HOW: In a new function, loop over StratifiedKFold folds, for each fold: create DataLoaders, train DeBERTa with gradient accumulation (accumulate gradients over 2 batches before optimizer step), use cosine LR scheduler (torch.optim.lr_scheduler.CosineAnnealingLR with warmup via linear increase), early stopping on val loss with patience 3. After all folds, concatenate OOF embeddings, train XGBoost and LR on combined features (handcrafted + OOF DeBERTa), and generate predictions as before. Preserve existing submission format and file paths."
  }
}"}
